project: lacc
target: lacc

# File-input (CLI) target: feed the fuzz bytes as a C source file to lacc — the small self-hosting
# C compiler that PARSES C and emits assembly. The whole front end (preprocessor, scanner, parser,
# decl/expr/stmt/type analysis) and the x86_64 backend run on the input. Not libFuzzer: the natural
# fuzz surface is the compiler on a source file. @@ is the input-file placeholder.
#
# -S stops after code generation (emit assembly) WITHOUT invoking an external assembler/linker, and
# -o /dev/null discards the asm — so the target exercises lacc's own parse+codegen, not gas/ld.
# Ported from the old integration's file-input target (old: /lacc/bin/lacc on a fixed /test.c path).
#
# -x c FORCES the input language to C. lacc (a C-compiler driver) otherwise infers the language from
# the file's EXTENSION (only `.c`/`.i` => C; see add_input_file() in src/lacc.c). Mayhem's @@ input
# file has NO extension, so without -x c lacc leaves it LANG_UNKNOWN and aborts with
# "Unrecognized input file" (exit 1) on EVERY input — including valid C and the default seed — so
# Mayhem could never obtain a passing test case. lacc has no usable stdin ('-') input mode, so -x c
# is the fix. With -x c a valid C program compiles to exit 0 (passing test) and the bytes are parsed.
cmds:
    # abort explicit so any sanitizer finding terminates the process (SIGABRT) and is recorded as a crash.
  - cmd: /mayhem/lacc -x c -S @@ -o /dev/null
